  {
    "item": 20,
    "thesis": {
      "description": "How much of Nvidia's $25 billion of equity-investment commitments outstanding at July 26, 2026 will be funded or remain outstanding at its next annual report?",
      "outcome": "The remaining balance of Nvidia's equity-investment commitments, the cash paid to fund them, and any change in the committed amounts, as disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.",
      "period": "Nvidia's next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027), as fixed by the reason for an obligation already undertaken.",
      "opens_as": "binding_obligation",
      "settled_by": "Nvidia's annual report on Form 10-K for fiscal 2027, including the commitments/contractual-obligations note and the investing section of the cash-flow statement."
    },
    "article": {
      "publication_date": "2026-10-12",
      "title": "Nvidia\u2019s Equity Investments Consume Half Its Free Cash Flow. Can the Payout Pace Last?",
      "text": "Nvidia passed our low-debt and strong-cash-flow screen. See the other businesses that cleared those financial hurdles alongside it . The chip business generates substantial cash, but shareholders also need to account for money invested in other companies. That spending sits outside the usual free-cash-flow calculation and can change how much remains for capital returns.\nNVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) reported August 26 that second-quarter revenue reached $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year. Paid demand for its computing infrastructure supports the AI investment case. The narrower question is whether cash from that business covers both ecosystem investments and shareholder distributions.\nThe investment bill sits below free cash flow\nOver the first half of fiscal 2027, operating cash flow was $74.421 billion. Subtracting $4.434 billion of equipment and intangible-asset purchases plus $92 million of related principal payments leaves $69.895 billion of company-defined free cash flow. That is a useful cash measure, but it leaves out $42.404 billion spent purchasing equity securities. Sales of equity securities brought in $7.241 billion, making the net investment outflow $35.163 billion.\nAfter that net investment spending, the same operating-cash base leaves $34.732 billion. Nvidia paid $39.044 billion for repurchases and $6.290 billion in dividends, a combined $45.334 billion. Net equity purchases consumed 50.3% of free cash flow. The resulting $10.602 billion gap required other funding on this narrow calculation. Debt issuance supplied $24.896 billion of cash; existing liquidity and other cash movements also matter. Our Check Point analysis examines when strong liquidity and a buyback program depend on financing beyond cash produced by the business .\nThis bridge includes actual cash paid, not the entire value of Nvidia's investment portfolio. It also leaves acquisitions, employee-tax payments and other uses outside the calculation. The $99 billion equity-investment balance at July 26 measures an asset position; it is not a bill payable again next quarter.\nNvidia also disclosed $25 billion of equity-investment commitments at July 26. Its August data-center guarantees carry a conditional aggregate cap of $105 billion, with exposure beginning as facilities enter service, expected from fiscal 2029. Those future and contingent amounts belong outside the current cash bridge. Our earlier portfolio analysis follows the investment risks linking Nvidia, Intel and CoreWeave .\nHolding equipment spending, net equity investment and distributions constant, operating cash would need to rise 14.2% to close the identified gap. Alternatively, net equity purchases would need to fall to $24.561 billion. These are funding thresholds, not forecasts. Investment sales and repurchases are discretionary, while commitments can constrain future flexibility.\nThe bull case is that ecosystem investments broaden computing adoption and support future product demand. Nvidia's growing sales already provide a substantial cash engine. The bear case is that weaker customer funding or deployment delays could hurt both chip collections and the companies in its investment portfolio.\nAt October 9 prices, Nvidia traded at 43.6 times reported trailing free cash flow. That denominator excludes equity purchases, so it cannot also be treated as cash entirely available for distributions. Insider Monkey counted 285 Nvidia holders in Q2 2026, up from 275 in Q1; Fisher increased its shares 3%. Those positions predate the August results. The next cash statement should show whether operating collections are keeping pace with the full capital-allocation program."
    },
    "fiscal_year_note": "NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-10-12 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.",
    "ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it": {
