      "not_judged": null,
      "reference": null,
      "favourable_end": null,
      "favourable_why": null,
      "ruled_out_by": null,
      "due_day_basis": null
    }
  },
  {
    "item": 39,
    "thesis": {
      "description": "Will Nvidia's Rubin data center GPU \u2014 the next generation of the product that carries Nvidia's data center revenue \u2014 reach volume ramp in the quarter management describes, or slip into calendar 2027 because of memory shortages?",
      "outcome": "Whether Rubin's volume ramp and shipments occur in the quarter Nvidia describes (about 20% of data center revenue) or are pushed into calendar 2027, and whether reported sales timing slips.",
      "period": "Check date: Nvidia's fiscal Q3 FY2027 report (~November 2026) and fiscal Q4 FY2027 report (~February 2027), which will show whether the Rubin ramp landed by the quarter ending late January 2027 or slid into calendar 2027, the two points the item frames ('this quarter' versus 'slip into 2027').",
      "opens_as": "product_node",
      "settled_by": "Nvidia's quarterly revenue-mix and shipment disclosures in its fiscal Q3 and Q4 FY2027 earnings releases and calls, plus confirmed Rubin system shipments by server OEMs/ODMs."
    },
    "article": {
      "publication_date": "2026-10-07",
      "title": "Nvidia (NVDA): Is There Still Room to Run?",
      "text": "Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) went nowhere for most of a year, then broke out in August and now trades at a record high. Ken Fisher's Fisher Asset Management is Nvidia's largest hedge fund holder as of the second quarter, with a stake worth about $18.2 billion, or 5.42% of its portfolio, after raising it by 3% in the second quarter. Nvidia ranks second in our list of his 10 best AI stocks. Click here to see the other nine picks .\nBulls say demand is still growing faster than Nvidia can build chips. AI is moving from training models to running them for businesses and consumers, and every new agent and app adds to the computing power needed. Weighing the AI chip giants? Read our comparison of Nvidia and Broadcom to see which is the better buy now . Management expects about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 and says supply holds that number back. Its CEO has said growth would come close to 100% if Nvidia could make every chip customers want.\nNvidia is also tackling the worry that matters most to the market, which is money. It lined up large lenders, including Goldman Sachs, Blackstone and Apollo, to finance customers' data centers, and it offers to cover up to 25% of the loss if the chips lose value. Its CUDA software lets the same chips handle both training and inference, which helps them keep their value, and its revenue per gigawatt of data center capacity has risen with each chip generation, according to its CEO. Even Stanley Druckenmiller has said he regrets selling Nvidia too early. Read what he said in our list of his 10 best AI stocks . Management also recently announced a record increase to its share buyback.\nMargins are the first worry. Nvidia cut its gross margin outlook for fiscal 2028 to 72% to 73%, down from about 75%, because memory prices have risen faster than expected. Its newest chip, Rubin, should make up only about 20% of data center revenue this quarter, and industry reports say the ramp could slip into 2027 because of memory shortages. A delay would push sales out. Want more AI ideas? See our list of the 10 best AI stocks to buy before they explode .\nThe bigger risk sits with its customers. Big cloud companies burned cash in the second quarter, their debt is rising, and Morgan Stanley estimates the cash they generate will cover only part of the data center spending planned through 2028. Nvidia is helping fill that gap with guarantees and financing support, so part of its demand now depends on borrowing staying affordable while rates climb. A default by a large customer such as OpenAI would test those guarantees.\nCompetition is also growing. AMD is pushing harder, and cloud companies are building their own chips with partners like Broadcom. A startup backed by former Intel CEO Pat Gelsinger is also going after Nvidia's AI networking business. See how it plans to challenge Nvidia's edge . That is the risk for investors in Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA).\nNvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) trades at a forward P/E of 25.7, only slightly above the sector median of 23.8 and about 39% below its own five-year average of 42.2. Analysts expect earnings per share to rise 95% in fiscal 2027 and 70% in fiscal 2028, which puts the P/E at about 15 on fiscal 2028 earnings. The PEG ratio, which compares the P/E to growth, is 0.49 against 1.29 for the sector, so Nvidia costs less than half as much per point of growth as a typical stock in its sector. Analysts raised their fiscal 2028 estimate by about 24% over the past 90 days, and the stock looks undervalued for its growth, as long as Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) delivers on those forecasts."
    },
    "fiscal_year_note": "NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-10-07 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.",
    "ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it": {
      "object": "NVDA",
      "kind": "asset",
      "focus": "Whether Rubin's volume ramp and shipments occur in the quarter Nvidia describes (about 20% of data center revenue) or are pushed into calendar 2027, and whether reported sales timing slips.",
      "question": "Will Nvidia's Rubin data center GPU reach volume ramp by the end of fiscal Q3 FY2027?",
      "year": "2026",
      "due_day": "2026-10-31",
      "rungs": [
        {
          "label": "Yes",
          "condition": "Nvidia's Rubin data center GPU reaches volume ramp by October 31, 2026."
        },
        {
          "label": "Ruled out",
          "condition": "Nvidia formally abandons the Rubin data center GPU volume ramp after October 7, 2026. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-07 and stands on 2026-10-31, and only when rung 1 does not."
        },
        {
          "label": "Still open",
          "condition": "Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-10-31."
        }
      ]
    },
    "other_fields": {
      "not_judged": [
        {
          "part": "Whether the ramp slips into calendar 2027",
          "why": "The question assesses whether the ramp occurs by the end of the stated quarter, not its later timing."
        },
        {
          "part": "Whether memory shortages cause a delay",
          "why": "The cause of a delay is separate from whether the ramp occurs."
        }
      ],
      "reference": null,
      "favourable_end": null,
      "favourable_why": null,
      "ruled_out_by": "Nvidia",
      "due_day_basis": "period_end"
    }
  },
