ARTICLE Nvidia’s Equity Investments Consume Half Its Free Cash Flow. Can the Payout Pace Last? Nvidia passed our low-debt and strong-cash-flow screen. See the other businesses that cleared those financial hurdles alongside it . The chip business generates substantial cash, but shareholders also need to account for money invested in other companies. That spending sits outside the usual free-cash-flow calculation and can change how much remains for capital returns. NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) reported August 26 that second-quarter revenue reached $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year. Paid demand for its computing infrastructure supports the AI investment case. The narrower question is whether cash from that business covers both ecosystem investments and shareholder distributions. The investment bill sits below free cash flow Over the first half of fiscal 2027, operating cash flow was $74.421 billion. Subtracting $4.434 billion of equipment and intangible-asset purchases plus $92 million of related principal payments leaves $69.895 billion of company-defined free cash flow. That is a useful cash measure, but it leaves out $42.404 billion spent purchasing equity securities
